-- title:
--   Theorem 5.10, p. 16 — ∫L is a Kan simplicial Banach manifold and every map of the tower (28) is a Kan fibration
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be an $L_\infty$-algebra, non-negatively graded and finite dimensional in each degree, with Chevalley–Eilenberg differential $\delta$ on $C^*(L)$, and fix an integer $r \ge 1$ (forms of class $C^r$ with $C^r$ differential, Convention 5.1). Let $\int L$ be the integrating simplicial manifold, $(\int L)_m = \mathrm{Hom}_{DGA}(C^*(L), \Omega^*(\Delta^m))$. Then:
--
--   1. each $(\int L)_m$ is a smooth manifold, and $\int L$ satisfies the Kan condition (4): for all $m \ge 1$ and $0 \le j \le m$ the restriction $(\int L)_m \to \mathrm{Hom}(\Lambda[m,j], \int L)$ is a surjective submersion;
--   2. all the maps of the Postnikov tower
--   $$\cdots \to \textstyle\int\tau_{\le 1}L \to \int\tau_{<1}L \to \int\tau_{\le 0}L \to *\qquad (28)$$
--   are Kan fibrations, that is, $\int\tau_{\le n}L \to \int\tau_{<n}L$ for every $n \ge 0$ (for $n = 0$ the target is the point $\int\tau_{<0}L$) and $\int\tau_{<n+1}L \to \int\tau_{\le n}L$ for every $n \ge 0$.
--
--   This is the main theorem of the paper: it says that Sullivan's spatial realization, built with $C^r$ de Rham forms, integrates every such $L_\infty$-algebra to a Kan simplicial Banach manifold, a Lie $\infty$-group in the paper's sense, and that its Postnikov tower is a tower of Kan fibrations.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: “(∫L)ₘ is a smooth manifold”, structure not named (p. 16); the paper builds it as level sets in products of the Banach spaces Ωⁿ_{Cʳ} (pp. 11–13, 16–17) → (∫L)ₘ, and every horn object, is a C^∞ split submanifold (IsSplitSubmanifold) of an explicit Banach space: the generator images Amb r S m, resp. a product of such spaces; reason: this pins the Cʳ topology the paper uses; “some manifold structure exists” would be trivial (a discrete structure makes every map a submersion)
--   - encoding: an L∞-algebra: brackets [ ], [ , ], … of degrees −1, 0, 1, …, whose axioms are δ² = 0 for the induced derivation δ of C∗(L) (§1.1, p. 1); non-negatively graded, finite dimensional in each degree (p. 13) → L : ℕ → Type with FiniteDimensional ℝ (L i) for every i, and δ : LInfty L, a linear map of degree +1 on CE L that is a graded derivation with δ ∘ δ = 0; reason: §1.1: a degree-one derivation of C∗(L) with δ² = 0 is the same thing as the brackets with their axioms; no sign convention for the brackets is needed
--   - encoding: C∗(L) = Sym(L∨), graded-commutative, L∗ᵢ in degree i + 1 (§1.1, p. 1) → CE L = ExteriorAlgebra(⊕_{i even} L∗ᵢ) ⊗ SymmetricAlgebra(⊕_{i odd} L∗ᵢ), with the generator map gen and the degree pieces ceDeg; reason: generators of odd degree anticommute and those of even degree commute; the plain tensor product is the graded one because the symmetric factor is concentrated in even degrees
--   - encoding: the Postnikov pieces τ≤ₙL, τ<ₙL with the brackets inherited from L (Definition 5.6, p. 14), and ∫ of them → generator systems tauLe δ n, tauLt δ n: L∗ᵢ for i < n, kerSub δ n = ker ∂ₙ₊₁∗ resp. imSub δ n = im ∂ₙ∗ for i = n, 0 above (tauLt δ 0 = 0); ker ∂ₙ₊₁∗ = {ξ | δξ ∈ subalgebra generated by L∗_{≤n}}, im ∂ₙ∗ = {ξ | ∃ η ∈ L∗ₙ₋₁, δη − ξ ∈ subalgebra generated by L∗_{≤n−1}}; ∫ of a piece = DGA maps out of the subalgebra it generates; reason: the projection L → τL dualizes to an injective DGA map C∗(τL) → C∗(L) whose image is this subalgebra, since (coker ∂ₙ₊₁)∗ = ker ∂ₙ₊₁∗ and (im ∂ₙ)∗ ≅ (Lₙ/ker ∂ₙ)∗ = im ∂ₙ∗; the characterizations hold because the nonlinear part of δ on L∗ₙ only involves L∗_{≤n}, and they do not depend on the sign of the transpose
--   - encoding: de Rham forms on the m-simplex Δᵐ; Ωⁿ_{Cʳ}(M) = {α of class Cʳ | dα of class Cʳ} with its Banach topology (Convention 5.1, p. 13) → forms are ExteriorAlgebra((ℝᵐ)∗)-valued functions on ℝᵐ, only their values on the corner simplex {t ≥ 0, ∑ t ≤ 1} matter; d uses derivatives within Δᵐ; Ωⁿ_{Cʳ}(Δᵐ) = CrForms r m n, the closed span of the jets (all partial derivatives of order ≤ r of the coefficients of α and of dα, restricted to Δᵐ, sup norms); reason: this jet norm is equivalent to the Cʳ norm on α and dα; the jet image of these forms is closed, so the closed span is the space itself; faces are pullbacks along the affine cofaces of (3)
--   - encoding: (∫L)ₘ = Hom_DGA(C∗(L), Ω∗(Δᵐ)) (Definition 5.2, (20)) → integ r δ S m ⊆ Amb r S m: the tuples (φ(gen bₖ))_{i<m, k} of images of a chosen basis bₖ of each generator space S i, i < m, over the algebra maps φ from the subalgebra generated by S into forms that send L∗ᵢ to (i+1)-forms, take values in Ω∗_{Cʳ} and satisfy d ∘ φ = φ ∘ δ on Δᵐ (IsDGAMap); reason: a DGA map out of a free graded-commutative algebra is determined by the images of its generators, and generators of degree > m go to 0 on Δᵐ; the choice of basis changes no topology
--   - encoding: submersion: every point has a neighbourhood U of its image and a section U → M through it (Definition 4.1, p. 11); covers of Banach manifolds are the surjective submersions (Corollary 4.5) → IsSurjSubmersionOn κ M N: κ maps M onto N and every x ∈ M has an open U ∋ κ x of the ambient space and a C^∞ map s on U with s (κ x) = x and s y ∈ M, κ (s y) = y for y ∈ U ∩ N; reason: for a split submanifold N, smooth maps on U ∩ N are exactly the restrictions of smooth maps on ambient open sets (locally)
--   - encoding: Hom(Λ[m, j], X), and the object Hom(Λ[m, j] → Δ[m], X → Y) of commutative squares (10) (Definitions 1.2, 2.3) → compatible tuples (xᵢ)_{i≠j} of (m−1)-simplices with dₐ x_{b+1} = d_b xₐ for a ≤ b (HornCompat), resp. such a tuple of points of ∫_S with a point y of (∫_{S'})ₘ and dᵢ y = res xᵢ (RelHorn), as subsets of products of the ambient Banach spaces; the Kan map is z ↦ ((dᵢ z)_{i≠j}, res z); reason: a map from the horn is a compatible family of maps from its facets; the face maps are restrictions along the cofaces
--   - encoding: Kan fibration (Definition 2.3): the map from Xₘ to the object of squares (10) is a cover, for all m ≥ 1 and 0 ≤ j ≤ m; Kan condition (4) = Kan fibration to ∗ → IsKanFibration r δ S S': S' ≤ S, all (∫_S)ₘ and (∫_{S'})ₘ are split submanifolds, and for all m ≥ 1, j the object of squares is a split submanifold and the map to it is a surjective submersion; the Kan condition is IsKanFibration r δ ⊤ ⊥; reason: the paper's notion presupposes that these objects are manifolds (Corollary 2.5); ∫_⊥ = ∗ since the zero generator system gives C∗ = ℝ
-- source:
--   Henriques, Integrating L∞-algebras, arXiv:math/0603563v2, p. 16, Theorem 5.10

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_IntLInfty_Kan_CE
import Definitions.Def_IntLInfty_Kan_Forms
import Definitions.Def_IntLInfty_Kan_Manifold
import Definitions.Def_IntLInfty_Kan_Integ

namespace IntLInfty.Kan

theorem theorem_5_10 (r : ℕ) (hr : 1 ≤ r) (L : ℕ → Type) [∀ i, AddCommGroup (L i)]
    [∀ i, Module ℝ (L i)] [∀ i, FiniteDimensional ℝ (L i)] (δ : LInfty L) :
    IsKanFibration r δ ⊤ ⊥ ∧
    (∀ n : ℕ, IsKanFibration r δ (tauLe δ n) (tauLt δ n)) ∧
    (∀ n : ℕ, IsKanFibration r δ (tauLt δ (n + 1)) (tauLe δ n)) := by sorry

end IntLInfty.Kan
